{"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are ThreadSense Advisor researcher. You research annotation-project guidance, decisions, announcements and member threads, and you never go beyond the evidence provided.\nYour personal goal is: Prepare one evidence-cited advice draft for a team lead and name the research it still needs."},{"role":"user","content":"\nCurrent Task: Revise your previous draft once, using the follow-up retrieval results now included in the evidence. Keep every claim cited; weaken or remove claims the evidence does not support. No further retrieval will run: leave remaining gaps with follow_up null. You are the ThreadSense Advisor preparing one topic for a team lead's morning brief. Use only the evidence provided and cite it by evidence_id. Member messages (origin member_message, with canonical author_id, author_role, thread_starter_id/thread_starter_role and reactions) are the only evidence for what members report. People: refer to them by role, never by name (\"a tutor\", \"a QA tutor\", \"the team lead\", \"an engineer\", \"the thread starter\"), in every summary, claim, rationale and action. Use singular wording for one person; plural wording (\"three tutors report\") only when that many distinct authors are cited. In each claim, list in attributions whom the role wording refers to (user_id, relation, cited evidence_id): AUTHOR wrote the cited message (reports, asks, confirms, instructs), THREAD_STARTER started that message's thread, PARTICIPANT only for a claim that the person took part in the thread. Quotations are copied unchanged. INCIDENT_FACT claims state what members report (\"a tutor reports ...\"), never a verified cause; a CAUSE claim needs a retrieved record or a team lead or engineer message that verifies it. A team lead's instruction inside a member thread is a THREAD_INSTRUCTION claim citing that message: it applies to that thread's scope and is not project guidance. A request or question from anyone else (member messages with instruction_authority NONE: an engineer, QA tutor or tutor) is an INCIDENT_FACT naming who asked (\"an engineer asks for the error state on task X\"): a reported request with no authority; it may still be a premise. Retrieved sources support guidance and history according to their \"use\": CURRENT_GUIDANCE may support what applies now; ENACTED_DECISION is a team lead decision whose enactment as policy is on record: it may support what applies now within its recorded scope, and its status line names the governing source (cite that announcement or guideline too when it is in the evidence); PRIOR_DECISION and HISTORY_ONLY explain earlier decisions, outcomes or old rules and must not be presented as current guidance (an approved, posted or executed decision is still a prior record unless its use is ENACTED_DECISION); withdrawn guidance has no replacement and does not apply; UNRESOLVED_VERSION must not be resolved by you. ANNOUNCEMENT items include routine reminders, schedules and example-collection notes: before a GUIDANCE claim relies on one, list it in announcement_assessments as relevant (same project, batch and problem) and directive (it tells people what to do), quoting the directing sentence verbatim; otherwise mark it not relevant or not directive. An announcement governs only what it directs: a request to post examples in a thread governs where examples go, not escalation or an engineering route. To say what any retrieved source says without relying on it as current handling, write a SOURCE_FACT claim citing it (\"the batch announcement asks for examples in its thread\"); it keeps the source's status and dates and establishes no procedure, authority or destination. Read each excerpt with its status line, dates, scope, authority, authorship and recorded states. Authorship is separate from authority: authorship.author_id is the verified author (a Slack record's message author); author_id absent with verified false means the record names no author, and its authority actor (actor_relation RECORD_AUTHORITY) approved, decided or recorded it without being its author. Write \"the earlier handoff records ...\" rather than naming who wrote it, and list an AUTHOR attribution for a retrieved record only when its authorship is verified. A quote with quote_kind VERBATIM must be copied exactly from one cited excerpt; otherwise set quote_kind PARAPHRASE. Coverage: before advising, fill coverage for CURRENT_GUIDANCE, ANNOUNCEMENTS, PRIOR_DECISIONS (earlier decisions, trials and their outcomes) and MEMBER_QUESTIONS (what members asked, including references to earlier workarounds or discussions). COVERED cites the evidence; NOT_APPLICABLE says why none is needed; MISSING names a gap whose follow_up query (source_types; version_mode history for past decisions, outcomes, recurrence or supersession) could find it. Request follow-ups only for support your advice actually needs; a nonempty result list is not sufficient evidence. Do not invent causes, outcomes, approvals, counts or fixes. Action: when the evidence supports a concrete step (post or align on current guidance, escalate, route to a specialist, update documentation), propose it with support_claim_ids naming its premises: the claims the instruction, draft and scope note rely on, including any destination or condition (a judge will decide which are essential); claims only the rationale draws on (why another route or source does not apply, background) go in context_claim_ids; it may be proposed even though the card will need team lead review. Recipients and routes: keep apart what an action recommends, the functional recipient it proposes (engineering, a specialist, a QA reviewer) and any concrete route it names (a specific thread, channel, person or queue it goes to or through). A proposed functional recipient is justified by supported incident evidence showing a problem that needs that recipient's expertise; evidence of an established intake route is not required, and mentioning the recipient justifies nothing by itself. A concrete route, or a claim that a route is designated, official or mandatory, is a claim of its own and needs a premise whose evidence establishes it; an example-collection or status note asks people where to post examples and does not make that thread an intake route. The recommendation stays advisory (the team lead decides; it need not be shown to be the required or only next step): when no evidence names the concrete route, leave it unspecified (\"report it to engineering\") rather than choosing one. Any action other than NO_ACTION states intervention_need: the kind (UNRESOLVED_HANDLING_QUESTION, GUIDANCE_GAP, APPLICABLE_PENDING_DECISION or OPERATIONAL_PROBLEM), one sentence on why intervening now is needed, and the evidence that shows it. Infer the need from what the threads and records show, not from keywords; nobody has to ask for intervention or say work is blocked. Handling questions that a team lead or QA tutor already answered in the thread are not unresolved; repeated questions across threads that still lack an answer, or answers that disagree, can be. Existing guidance does not by itself mean NO_ACTION, and \"a reminder might help\" is not a need. If the evidence shows no such need (for example an observation the team lead already handled or deliberately kept open), propose NO_ACTION. That a team lead approves or executes the action is not an open question. Set open_confirmation OPEN only for a specific question that the cited guidance and decisions leave unsettled, state it in open_question, and cite in open_question_evidence_ids the evidence showing it is unsettled; otherwise NONE. It must be a handling or policy question the team lead has to decide or confirm (for example which of two current rules applies, or whether an earlier decision covers these cases). A technical cause not yet diagnosed, diagnostic results not yet collected, an expert's assessment not yet given, generic uncertainty, and earlier review flags (already carried with the card) are not open confirmations. A confirmation that current guidance assigns to the team lead is one once its required inputs are available; while inputs the team lead requested are still missing, say so instead. action_choice: fill it only when the evidence leaves a real choice between intervening and observing, or between materially different interventions (e.g. a reminder versus an escalation): name the competing action types and the tradeoff the evidence does not settle, citing it. Imagining an alternative, an earlier review flag, or a root cause nobody knows yet is not such a choice; otherwise null. Severity: apply the rubric unchanged; MEDIUM or HIGH must cite a rubric signal by level, index and exact text, supported by member messages; affected_reporters lists only people whose own message (cite that message) reports being affected, not everyone who replied. recurrence FOUND means historical recurrence corroborated by retrieved records (cite them in recurrence_evidence_ids); members saying it is happening again go in reported_recurrence_evidence_ids, which is a report, not corroborated history. Resolution: give each member a state (UNRESOLVED, PARTIALLY_RESOLVED, RESOLVED_IN_THREAD or UNKNOWN) from that member's own complete thread, citing the messages that show it; Agent 1's resolution label is an attributed input, often unverified, and is not evidence; one member's resolution says nothing about another member. UPDATE_DOCUMENTATION and UPDATE_FAQ name targets (evidence_id, section, ADD for a missing entry, CORRECT or REMOVE for stale wording); target only what the evidence shows is missing or stale. Severity, confidence, resolution and evidence sufficiency are separate judgments. List source_conflicts between evidence items that disagree. If no evidence addresses the topic, set evidence_sufficiency UNSUPPORTED and propose no operational action. Upstream review flags stay open; you cannot clear them. Everything that reaches the card is checked statement by statement against the full topic context. Card writing. The team lead reads these fields exactly as you write them. A card has two levels: closed, it shows the summary and the action's instruction; opened, it adds the detail sentences, the rationale, the draft and what to check. Give each field one purpose. summary_sentences (the closed card: the situation and the important point that is still open or already settled, usually two sentences and about 35-55 words in all, up to 3 sentences when facts with different evidence need them; leave summary null: it is their text joined); leave reporter-by-reporter breakdowns, policy explanations and earlier records to detail_sentences unless the situation cannot be understood without them. detail_sentences (the opened card's \"What happened\", up to 6 short sentences; empty when the summary already says everything useful): the distinct symptoms with the roles and counts that reported each and their task references or threads; the requests already made (who asked, in which threads) and the information still missing; earlier records, described as earlier records and kept apart from current reports. Do not repeat a summary sentence or list every message. Each summary and detail sentence is judged and shown on its own, so make each one complete; list the claims it condenses in support_claim_ids and the earlier sentences it refers back to (\"this\", \"their questions\"): depends_on gives positions (1, 2, ...) of earlier sentences at the same level, and summary_depends_on (detail sentences only) positions of summary sentences. A sentence says nothing its claims do not support, and one unsupported part withholds the whole sentence: give facts with different evidence their own sentences (what members report; current handling or what the team lead has asked for; what earlier records show) rather than joining unrelated facts to stay within two sentences. A sentence whose subject is several people (\"three tutors ask ...\") states only what each of them asked or reported: when their questions or reports differ, say how (\"a QA tutor asks X; two others ask Y\") or use wording that covers each one, as the claims do. proposed_action.instruction (one concrete sentence telling the team lead what to do, about 15-25 words); proposed_action.rationale (why the evidence and the applicable guidance make this step fit, 1-2 short sentences, judged and shown on its own; do not retell the incident, which belongs in detail_sentences, or give instructions to recipients, which belong in the draft; for NO_ACTION, say why no further intervention is needed now, from the evidence; the reason is judged and shown only if supported); proposed_action.scope_note (only conditions and limits the action depends on); proposed_action.draft_text (a copyable draft suited to the action: an announcement of about 60-120 words, with bullets when useful, or the message to send for an escalation or referral; when it covers several reports, group them by the symptom they share, with counts and roles (\"two tutors and a QA tutor report a grey Submit button after choosing B; a tutor reports that clicking Submit does nothing on RP-1577\"), rather than one \"another tutor reports ...\" clause per report: group only reports whose cited messages state that symptom, keep each task reference with its own report, and keep every distinct symptom and who reported it); review_note (only for a specific check the team lead still has to make that the card does not already list, and which threads or sources it concerns, for example requested details that are still missing; the card itself lists high severity, the open upstream review flags and their count, withheld statements and the open question, so do not restate those; otherwise null). State a diagnostic request or a missing detail once, where it serves its purpose (what is still missing in detail_sentences, what recipients should provide in the draft); repeat it in the instruction, rationale, scope note or review note only when that field needs it for a different purpose. A draft the team lead will post is in the team lead's own voice and addressed to its recipients: tell them what to provide or do; do not refer to the team lead in the third person or include steps only the team lead takes (those belong in the instruction). Put policy wording in a draft only when it helps the recipients act, and then at the strength and scope of the governing source it comes from (keep its conditions, exceptions and words such as \"usually\" or \"can\"), listing that source's claim among the action's premises; a participant's explanation or suggestion may be attributed to them (\"a QA tutor suggests ...\") but is not stated as a rule unless a current guideline, FAQ or established announcement states it. Keep requests apart by who made them and where: a request made in specific threads or about specific tasks stays with those threads and tasks, separate from a request the team lead made in every thread. When no evidence names a delivery route and the action does not depend on one, leave the route unspecified without commenting on its absence in any of these fields. These are writing targets, not limits: keep necessary conditions, uncertainty, scope and who reported, asked, confirmed or instructed. Plain wording applies to every field the team lead reads, including severity.rationale and each rubric signal's support: prefer familiar words when they keep the meaning (\"prompt requirements\" for \"prompt constraints\", \"compare the QA decisions\" for \"calibrate\", \"change how ties are judged\" for \"change the tie threshold\"), keep a project term when a plainer one would change its meaning, quote sources exactly, and leave out processing phrases such as \"in the supplied batch context\" or \"inferred from the topic's member entries\". Use familiar words: \"post a reminder\", not \"post alignment\"; \"report a technical issue\", not \"tool-incident reporting\". Never put evidence IDs, claim IDs, user IDs, field or enum names, rubric references, premise lists or processing details in these fields or in claim text; the evidence mapping belongs in evidence_ids, support_claim_ids, attributions and targets. Use double quotation marks only for exact quotations copied unchanged from a cited source. Not every topic needs an announcement, or any action. Summaries and claims keep apart: what several reporters have in common versus a symptom only some of them report (\"three tutors report X; one also reports Y\"); a member asking a question versus a member reporting being affected; and a reported symptom versus a confirmed finding. Attribute each detail only to the roles whose cited messages state it. When research found nothing, say what this run retrieved (\"the records retrieved for this run do not show ...\"); never state that no record, decision or outcome exists anywhere.\n\nInput JSON:\n{\"topic\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"batch_id\": \"multi_batch_11\", \"candidate_group_id\": \"cluster_afc9cd285daaec7f\", \"topic_id\": \"cluster_afc9cd285daaec7f/topic_1\", \"topic_label\": \"Spanish formal vs informal address alignment\", \"projection_gap\": null, \"as_of\": \"2026-10-02T12:00:00Z\", \"member_ids\": [\"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"member_d28a821e7d752b0e76cb6d63\"]}, \"member_evidence\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"thread_id\": \"1790896116.000178\", \"author_id\": \"U019\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T23:08:36.000178Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U019\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Should formal address always be preferred in ES?\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"thread_id\": \"1790896116.000178\", \"author_id\": \"U017\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T23:09:34.000113Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U019\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"The response begins with usted then addresses the same user as tú. We need to know whether the user requested formal address explicitly.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"thread_id\": \"1790896116.000178\", \"author_id\": \"U007\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T23:10:13.000226Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U019\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Can Spanish QA examine whether tú is used for a different addressee? Please keep the full Spanish exchange; isolated pronouns do not establish tone.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"thread_id\": \"1790896116.000178\", \"author_id\": \"U001\", \"author_role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-01T23:10:58.000339Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U019\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Please provide the exact before/after sentences and the target audience before choosing the penalty.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"THREAD_SCOPE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_d28a821e7d752b0e76cb6d63\", \"thread_id\": \"1790910015.000180\", \"author_id\": \"U017\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T03:00:15.000180Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U017\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"reactions\": [{\"name\": \"eyes\", \"users\": [\"U001\"]}], \"excerpt\": \"Spanish register changes without the user requesting informality.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_d28a821e7d752b0e76cb6d63\", \"thread_id\": \"1790910015.000180\", \"author_id\": \"U007\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T03:01:03.000113Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U017\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Can Spanish QA examine whether tú is used for a different addressee?\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}], \"agent1_interpretations\": [{\"member_id\": \"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"thread_id\": \"1790896116.000178\", \"summary\": \"Yuki asks whether formal address should always be preferred in Spanish (ES). Bea notes that a response begins with 'usted' but then switches to 'tú', indicating a need to know if the user explicitly requested formal address. Leo suggests that Spanish QA should check if 'tú' is used for a different addressee and emphasizes that isolated pronouns do not determine tone. Alex, a TEAM_LEAD, requests the exact before/after sentences and the target audience before deciding on a penalty.\", \"central_question\": \"Should formal address always be preferred in ES?\", \"attributed_agent1_resolution\": {\"state\": \"UNRESOLVED\", \"rationale\": \"The thread discusses the use of formal versus informal address in Spanish, with various contributors providing insights and questions. However, the original question about whether formal address should always be preferred remains unresolved as no definitive guidance is provided. All replies request further diagnostic information or clarification and do not provide a substantive answer to the question.\", \"agent1_verification_status\": \"CORRECTED\", \"note\": \"Agent 1 label, not evidence; NEEDS_REVIEW means Agent 1 did not verify it. Assess resolution from the member messages.\"}, \"agent1_review_required\": false, \"agent1_review_reason\": \"\", \"note\": \"Agent 1 interpretation, not source evidence; cite member messages for incident facts.\"}, {\"member_id\": \"member_d28a821e7d752b0e76cb6d63\", \"thread_id\": \"1790910015.000180\", \"summary\": \"Bea raises a concern about Spanish register changes occurring without user-requested informality. Leo suggests that Spanish QA should investigate whether the informal 'tú' is used for a different addressee.\", \"central_question\": \"Are Spanish register changes occurring without user-requested informality?\", \"attributed_agent1_resolution\": {\"state\": \"UNRESOLVED\", \"rationale\": \"The thread does not establish a substantive answer to the question about Spanish register changes. While Bea raises the issue, and Leo proposes an examination by Spanish QA, no conclusions or decisions are made regarding the original concern.\", \"agent1_verification_status\": \"VERIFIED\", \"note\": \"Agent 1 label, not evidence; NEEDS_REVIEW means Agent 1 did not verify it. Assess resolution from the member messages.\"}, \"agent1_review_required\": false, \"agent1_review_reason\": \"\", \"note\": \"Agent 1 interpretation, not source evidence; cite member messages for incident facts.\"}], \"retrieved_evidence\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"multilingual-eval-faq@1.1\", \"title\": \"Multilingual Evaluation — FAQ (v1.1)\", \"sections\": [\"Register and honorifics\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"multilingual-eval-faq\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"faq\", \"record_kind\": \"faq\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-18T13:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-18T13:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U001\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Register and honorifics\\n\\n### Q: Who scores Korean or Japanese register shifts?\\nRoute the case to appropriate Korean or Japanese language QA with exact sentence endings and surrounding context. Check quotations and addressee changes. The task-quality grade depends on demonstrated impact; do not assign a major penalty from the presence of a shift alone.\\n\\n### Q: Spanish tú versus usted inconsistency — major or minor?\\nAsk Spanish QA to check addressee, intended formality and discourse context. An unjustified shift can be an error, but its grade needs a case-specific rationale.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:multilingual-eval-instructions@1.2:9199a335\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"multilingual-eval-instructions@1.2\", \"title\": \"Multilingual Evaluation — Instructions (v1.2)\", \"sections\": [\"Specialist routing\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"multilingual-eval-instructions\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"guideline\", \"record_kind\": \"guideline\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-10T13:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-10T13:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U001\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Specialist routing\\nUse the language being evaluated to select appropriate expertise. Spanish expertise does not establish Korean or Japanese expertise. If the prompt does not establish a required locale, seek clarification rather than inventing one.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"ANNOUNCEMENT\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"multi-locale-announcement@1.0\", \"title\": \"Locale and register routing announcement\", \"source\": {\"channel_id\": \"C_MULTI_ANNOUNCEMENTS\", \"message_id\": \"1790082000.700003\", \"system\": \"slack\", \"thread_id\": \"1790082000.700003\"}}, \"source_type\": \"announcement\", \"record_kind\": \"channel_announcement\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-22T13:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-22T13:00:00Z\", \"created_at\": \"2026-09-22T13:00:00.700003+00:00\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"batch_id\": \"multi_batch_11\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"same_batch\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U001\", \"actor_relation\": \"MESSAGE_AUTHOR\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_slack_archive\"}, \"authorship\": {\"author_id\": \"U001\", \"verified\": true, \"basis\": \"SLACK_MESSAGE_AUTHOR\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"tl_approved\", \"decision_status\": \"tl_approved\", \"execution_status\": \"posted\", \"outcome_status\": \"not_observed\"}, \"excerpt\": \"[ANNOUNCEMENT posted 2026-09-22T13:00:00Z by TEAM_LEAD (multi_batch_11): not established as handling for this topic until you show it is relevant and gives a directive.]\\nMultilingual batch 11: confirm the requested audience or locale before penalizing regional wording. Natural Southern Vietnamese vocabulary is not an error solely because a reviewer prefers a Northern term. Provide the exact phrase and prompt context. Send Korean cases to Hana, Japanese cases to Yuki, Vietnamese cases to Linh and Spanish cases to Leo. For register shifts, include the addressee and any quotation; an apparent shift is not an automatic major penalty.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval@1.0:bba64f57\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"PRIOR_DECISION\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval@1.0\", \"title\": \"Approve a small Spanish audit slot\", \"sections\": [\"Evidence available at review\", \"Prior ThreadSense advice\", \"TL response\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"decision\", \"record_kind\": \"advice_review\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\", \"enactment\": {\"status\": \"NOT_ENACTED\", \"missing\": [\"NOT_A_POLICY_CHANGE\", \"NO_ENACTMENT_EVIDENCE\"]}}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-21T16:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-21T16:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"batch_id\": \"multi_batch_11\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"same_batch\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U001\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"tl_approved\", \"decision_status\": \"tl_approved\", \"execution_status\": \"pending_scheduling\", \"outcome_status\": \"not_observed\"}, \"excerpt\": \"[PRIOR RECORD (advice_review; decision_status: tl_approved; execution_status: pending_scheduling; outcome_status: not_observed) - history, not a standing rule.]\\n# Approve a small Spanish audit slot\\n\\n## Evidence available at review\\n\\nThe weekly coordination log shows twelve Spanish tasks awaiting routine second review. Leo (U007, Spanish QA) offered a 30-minute slot on September 23. This was a scheduling backlog, with no recorded claim of widespread incorrect scoring or a new language defect.\\n\\nThe requested sample is ten tasks, selected across the participating tutors; the remaining two stay in the normal audit queue. Selection and the calendar booking are not yet recorded. Completion of this review does not certify the whole batch.\\n\\n## Prior ThreadSense advice\\n\\nBook Leo for a 30-minute Spanish audit discussion and assemble a ten-task sample from the September 18 queue, retaining the reviewer rationales.\\n\\n## TL response\\n\\nApproved. Use the offered September 23 slot after Leo confirms the booking. Sample across tutors rather than choosing only disagreements. Record which ten tasks were reviewed and leave the other two visible in the queue. This is routine audit coverage, not a change to scoring guidance.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:multi-audit-filename-convention-approval@1.0:a5a80940\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"PRIOR_DECISION\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"multi-audit-filename-convention-approval@1.0\", \"title\": \"Approve English field names for the audit index\", \"sections\": [\"Evidence available at review\", \"Prior ThreadSense advice\", \"TL response\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"multi-audit-filename-convention-approval\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"decision\", \"record_kind\": \"advice_review\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\", \"enactment\": {\"status\": \"NOT_ENAC